        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Ainu</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ainu Mosir, “the land of the Ainu”: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuril-islands">Kuril Islands</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, with communities in Kamchatka and Khabarovsk Krai; centres today include Nibutani and the Hidaka region of Hokkaido.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Ainu culture period, or Nibutani period, from about the 13th century to the present, after the Epi-Jōmon (from the 5th century BCE) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsumon-culture">Satsumon culture</a> (to about the 13th century).</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">kotan river villages; salmon fishing and deer and bear hunting; the Iyomante bear ceremony; belief in kamuy and inau offering sticks; attus bark-fibre robes and Ainu patterns; women’s tattoos; the Yukar epics; a language isolate</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ainu oral literature and Ainu voices today; Chinese records such as the History of Yuan and the Ming Veritable Records; Japanese and Russian records; the writings of John Batchelor and Bronisław Piłsudski; archaeology; genetic studies.</span></div>
        </div>
