    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Ainu</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hokkaido.jpg" alt="Hokkaido" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Hokkaido <span class="kid-tale-cr">663highland, CC BY 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Ainu are the Indigenous people of northern Japan and nearby parts of Russia.</p><p>They call their homeland Ainu Mosir, and their name means “human being”.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/okhotsk-culture.jpg" alt="Okhotsk culture" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Okhotsk culture <span class="kid-tale-cr">Ozizo, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their culture took shape about 800 years ago, from older cultures of the north.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millet.jpg" alt="Millet" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Millet <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kurt Stüber [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They lived in small villages called kotan, beside rivers where salmon swam upstream.</p><p>They fished for salmon, hunted deer and bears, and grew millet.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lacquerware.jpg" alt="Lacquerware" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Lacquerware <span class="kid-tale-cr">Hiart, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>For the Ainu, everything in nature holds a kamuy, a spirit or god.</p><p>Lacquer bowls from Honshu were treasured and used to offer drink to the kamuy.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>They wove robes from tree bark, and women wore necklaces of glass beads.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ainu.jpg" alt="Ainu" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Ainu <span class="kid-tale-cr">Torbenbrinker, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Later, Japanese rulers took much of their land and banned their language and religion.</p><p>Today Japan’s law recognises them as an Indigenous people, and craftswomen still weave traditional clothes.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Ainu, whose name means “human being”, are the Indigenous people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a> and the Kurils; their culture grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsumon-culture">Satsumon culture</a> with contributions from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okhotsk-culture">Okhotsk culture</a>.</li><li>Living in small river villages called kotan, they fished for salmon, hunted deer and bears and traded between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> and the Asian mainland, in a world where everything held a kamuy.</li><li>Japanese colonisation brought land seizure, forced labour, family separation, epidemics and bans on language and religion; a court first recognised the Ainu as an Indigenous people in 1997, and the Diet passed an act recognising them in 2019.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When Russians and Japanese met at trading posts in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a> in 1778–1779, the talks were held in the Ainu language, with Ainu guides translating for both sides.</p></aside>
    </section>

